#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116153} :activity/id #uuid "b08a34fb-70a3-5629-9b6a-f9a78ef3fef7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050962 :topic/id #uuid "75aea66f-2cbf-5c78-849b-d3faa857b9b4" :topic/math-academy-id 1585} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116505 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=f(-x)?$\n\n![](images/51/517dbe715d1f1e79756e6e7bd0d733cc5894f425ac02b2ac1bde2c2a33e86c8d.png)\n\nWe can use some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(-x).$\n\nFor example, let's take the point $(3,4)$. The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means $f(3)=4.$ Then, if $x={\\color{red}-3},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f\\left(-({\\color{red}-3})\\right) = f(3) = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis means the point $({\\color{red}3},4)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $({\\color{red}-3},4)$ that lies on $y=f(-x).$\n\nSo, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x),$ there will be a point on the graph of $y=f(-x)$ with the *same* $y$-value and the *opposite* $x$-value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(-x)[/math]} \\\\ (-1,2) & (1,2) \\\\ (0,-1) & (0,-1) \\\\ (2,0) & (-2,0) \\\\ (3,4) & (-3,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plot the points and draw the graph of $y = f(-x){:}$\n\n![](images/3a/3ae62ef6496a598d27e485990ab0d3b3cb6f23211de4a015d54614db463bb131.png)\n\nLooking back at the graph, we see that every point on the graph of $y=f(-x)$ is the **reflection across the $y$-axis** of a point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nSo, the graph of $y=f(-x)$ can be obtained by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the $y$-axis. This result holds in general." :tutorial/id #uuid "333ac18d-4b7d-5c9b-b443-fb3a705515ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d346ae0a-17ad-5edb-b7f6-751cd8a3e2e7" :step/math-academy-id 18023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115262 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115091 :question/id #uuid "8bbf173b-820a-5b50-b40f-72e4ef09267a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4977"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ecd0f74-19c3-5770-b376-4d945320f06f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575594}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of a Horizontally Reflected Function"} :step/id #uuid "d6940736-3c57-5ee2-a4a0-e708da2a4b61" :step/math-academy-id 18028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115092 :question/id #uuid "5b49540d-4bcd-5ba9-b258-d6ad9828f0e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4981"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6912eee-a9ca-5a57-8564-d276afc29711" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575528}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point that Lies on a Transformed Function"} :step/id #uuid "b05811d8-83b0-566f-8237-c7f9fd933b5f" :step/math-academy-id 18030}] :activity/title "Horizontal Reflections of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115419}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575655} :activity/id #uuid "01a2564a-f932-5a36-9d89-4ac6479831eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050962 :topic/id #uuid "75aea66f-2cbf-5c78-849b-d3faa857b9b4" :topic/math-academy-id 1585} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446f5e6f-db8f-5475-8726-ca18e6b7a9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575069} {:answer/id #uuid "493f0ae7-cc01-57fc-91ef-cb782b6b7670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575074} {:answer/id #uuid "4f20dcdd-e642-5844-8b02-166600b6c2e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575083} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6cb8d7-7aae-5539-8ff7-d15820c1d709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575195} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe4a972-90eb-5f47-b61f-d94a3c696e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575195} :answer-field/id #uuid "70884a80-03f4-56de-8e4c-1d5cee316b31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05fcb668-c7a9-5251-bb06-4685eeabb241" :question/math-academy-id "q-26412" :question/problem "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The transformation $y=f(-x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $y$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(1,1)$ lies $1$ unit to the *right* from the $y$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected $1$ unit to the *left* from the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/e3/e39b804eef0617e89d43808793c844f1223a427493e8b6554cc7ad25477f7ddc.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point $(-1,1)$ must lie on the graph of $y=f(-x).$"} :step/id #uuid "1ff7f28d-3d91-572a-a913-cca7dbe41b38"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8cfe3a-742b-5138-bec1-a887c025cfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e3/e3f11b9844e97e08f69a73e5b9f2604ea23dbc15ad57531bd16d2c600c18dacb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575058} {:answer/id #uuid "70a0b2bf-64a0-5749-a16c-0b858bdb3c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6f/6fd44ce422068a54474c78a71571a5a4d36456e78674cc6a51d7c6250c315a07.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575155} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3f5159-04ed-590a-9cf5-2f45164d61ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3b/3b5a90ceece937a4bca3a197b6c25dcfcb654442b74c135ebf20eab0c64f30a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575227} {:answer/id #uuid "b0655ee9-e1fb-59d8-af28-6d6bf19a46f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6f/6fe1073ff6bed394eb4f67ca80f8bfceb6f2e20b5a26fe6422385ca219b642f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575263} {:answer/id #uuid "f83994c6-c926-595f-b7c1-a9b5de32732c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ca/cafe8b95cf241f03ef95ee32660c73d31c01e307e51bb01f21b0690afbade423.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575058} :answer-field/id #uuid "2283bfaa-2991-53c3-a073-8f5090e7f864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "96a48954-07a7-579b-872e-b1d2125e9254" :question/math-academy-id "q-16701" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dee325f7573921b78c49390ed758bb03198f72d228b1b70d7a1026822ce8940.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the graph of $y=f(-x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $y$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](